{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"find / -name '*apply_patch*' -type f 2>/dev/null | head -15; cat results/ideas/02_honest_nonlinear_score_controls_with_a_finite_sample_safety_track/program_1/program_spec.toml; cat results/ideas/02_honest_nonlinear_score_controls_with_a_finite_sample_safety_track/program_1/src/program.py; cat results/ideas/02_honest_nonlinear_score_controls_with_a_finite_sample_safety_track/program_1/src/METHOD.md; cat results/ideas/02_honest_nonlinear_score_controls_with_a_finite_sample_safety_track/program_1/src/study.py\"","aggregated_output":"/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/tool_choice_apply_patch_param.py\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/tool_choice_apply_patch.py\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/response_apply_patch_tool_call_output.py\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/response_apply_patch_tool_call.py\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/apply_patch_tool_param.py\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/apply_patch_tool.py\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/__pycache__/tool_choice_apply_patch_param.cpython-312.pyc\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/__pycache__/tool_choice_apply_patch.cpython-312.pyc\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/__pycache__/response_apply_patch_tool_call_output.cpython-312.pyc\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/__pycache__/response_apply_patch_tool_call.cpython-312.pyc\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/__pycache__/apply_patch_tool_param.cpython-312.pyc\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/openai/types/responses/__pycache__/apply_patch_tool.cpython-312.pyc\nschema_version = 1\ncode_root = \"src\"\n\n[entrypoint]\npath = \"program.py\"\nlanguage = \"python\"\ntype = \"script\"\n\n[copy]\nexclude = [\n  \"**/__pycache__/**\",\n  \"**/*.pyc\",\n  \".venv/**\",\n  \"node_modules/**\",\n  \".git/**\",\n  \".mypy_cache/**\",\n  \".pytest_cache/**\",\n  \".ruff_cache/**\",\n  \".tox/**\",\n  \".nox/**\",\n  \"dist/**\",\n  \"build/**\",\n]\ninclude = [\n]\n\n[integration]\nkind = \"generic\"\nnotes = \"\"\n\"\"\"Frozen-predictor Bernoulli population-mean inference, CPU/binary64 only.\n\nRPC v1: handle(request) returns a finite JSON object. Only the exact documented\nkeys are accepted in responses; do not return diagnostics, paths, or state flags.\nThe predictor and samples are immutable. Fit inference-side transforms only.\nNever pool labels across episodes. No external files, network, or extra labels.\n\nreset: {\"op\":\"reset\",\"seed\":nonnegative integer}, response {\"ok\":true}.\nAccept arbitrary seeds; they initialize RNG, never identify a workload/plane.\nClears all arrays, coefficients, results and randomness. Exactly once per episode.\n\ninput: {\"op\":\"input\",\"n\":integer>=2,\"N\":integer>=2,\"alpha\":0.05,\n\"dtype\":\"<f8\",\"encoding\":\"base64\",\"shape\":[2*n+N],\n\"total_bytes\":8*(2*n+N),\"chunk_count\":integer in [1,4],\n\"chunk_index\":integer in [0,chunk_count),\"offset\":integer,\"data\":base64 string}.\nThe logical byte stream concatenates Y_L[n],f_L[n],f_U[N] in this order.\nEach scalar is finite in [0,1]; Y_L is binary. n,N,alpha and metadata are\nplane-independent workload settings. No true mean, unlabeled labels, case name,\nbaseline, score, feedback or resource paths are supplied. Chunks are contiguous,\nordered, nonempty, at most 1 MiB decoded each, at most 4 MiB total. First input\nsets metadata; all later inputs repeat it exactly. No separate setup RPC is\nneeded: input #0 performs setup. Response {\"ok\":true,\"received_bytes\":integer}.\nAll four input calls are budgeted even when the actual value fits one chunk.\nAssembly is exclusively in candidate-owned RAM. No spill or shared paths.\n\ninfer: {\"op\":\"infer\"}, only after every input chunk, exactly once per episode.\nCommits the input stream and fits the actual method in one operation. Response\n{\"estimate\":finite number,\"lower\":finite number,\"upper\":finite number}, with\n0<=lower<=upper<=1 and 0<=estimate<=1. Closed intervals target E[Y] at 1-alpha.\nNo additional response fields. Endpoints need not contain the point estimate.\nAfter infer only reset is legal. No state export/import or extra probes exist.\nEvery complete message including envelope/framing must be <64 MiB (design cap\n8 MiB; endpoint/ack responses <16 KiB); keep input logical total <=4 MiB.\n\nCandidate: two-fold honest, clipped, regularized linear-spline score controls.\nAll knots and tuning use complementary labeled folds only. Unlabeled scores\nare split too; the pooling coefficient is N/(n+N). The RPC returns a stabilized Wald\ntrack for n>=16 and the labeled-only exact interval for n<16, a size-only rule.\nThe independently computed bounded empirical-Bernstein track is internal and\nnever used to select a narrower interval. Wald coverage is only asymptotic;\nneither cross-fitting nor a small-sample exact branch makes it finite-sample\nvalid at other sizes. No empirical coverage or width improvement is asserted.\n\"\"\"\nimport base64\nimport math\n\nimport numpy as np\n\n_state = None\n\n\nclass ScoreCalibrator:\n    \"\"\"Small ridge linear-spline family, tuned exclusively on its fit sample.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, nonlinear=True):\n        self.nonlinear = nonlinear\n\n    def fit(self, scores, outcomes):\n        train_scores = np.asarray(scores, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\n        train_outcomes = np.asarray(outcomes, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\n        self.intercept = float(np.mean(train_outcomes))\n        self.knots = np.empty(0)\n        self.coefficients = np.empty(0)\n        self.feature_mean = np.empty(0)\n        self.feature_scale = np.empty(0)\n        sample_count = train_scores.size\n        if sample_count < 4 or np.ptp(train_scores) < 1e-12:\n            return self\n        centered_outcomes = train_outcomes - self.intercept\n        best_gcv = float(np.mean(centered_outcomes**2) / (1.0 - 1.0 / sample_count)**2)\n        knot_families = [np.empty(0)]\n        if self.nonlinear and sample_count >= 16:\n            knot_families.append(np.unique(np.quantile(train_scores, [0.25, 0.5, 0.75])))\n        if self.nonlinear and sample_count >= 64:\n            knot_families.append(np.unique(np.quantile(train_scores, np.linspace(0.125, 0.875, 7))))\n        for knots in knot_families:\n            features = self._basis(train_scores, knots)\n            feature_mean = features.mean(axis=0)\n            feature_scale = features.std(axis=0)\n            feature_scale = np.maximum(feature_scale, 1e-8)\n            centered_features = (features - feature_mean) / feature_scale\n            gram = centered_features.T @ centered_features / sample_count\n            cross = centered_features.T @ centered_outcomes / sample_count\n            identity = np.eye(gram.shape[0])\n            for penalty in (0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1.0, 10.0):\n                coefficient = np.linalg.solve(gram + penalty * identity, cross)\n                residuals = centered_outcomes - centered_features @ coefficient\n                degrees = 1.0 + float(np.trace(np.linalg.solve(gram + penalty * identity, gram)))\n                denominator = max(1.0 - degrees / sample_count, 1.0 / sample_count)\n                gcv = float(np.mean(residuals**2) / denominator**2)\n                if gcv < best_gcv:\n                    best_gcv = gcv\n                    self.knots = knots.copy()\n                    self.coefficients = coefficient\n                    self.feature_mean = feature_mean\n                    self.feature_scale = feature_scale\n        return self\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _basis(scores, knots):\n        return np.column_stack((scores, np.maximum(scores[:, None] - knots[None, :], 0.0)))\n\n    def predict(self, scores):\n        scores = np.asarray(scores, dtype=float).reshape(-1)\n        if not self.coefficients.size:\n            return np.full(scores.size, self.intercept)\n        features = (self._basis(scores, self.knots) - self.feature_mean) / self.feature_scale\n        return self.intercept + features @ self.coefficients\n\n\ndef fit_score_calibrator(train_scores, train_outcomes):\n    calibrator = ScoreCalibrator()\n    calibrator.fit(train_scores.reshape(-1, 1), train_outcomes)\n    return calibrator\n\n\ndef bounded_eb_radius(sample, lower_bound, upper_bound, delta):\n    \"\"\"Conservative two-sided empirical Bernstein radius for bounded iid data.\"\"\"\n    sample = np.asarray(sample, dtype=float)\n    if not 0.0 < delta < 1.0 or lower_bound > upper_bound:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid empirical-Bernstein parameters\")\n    if sample.size < 2:\n        return float(upper_bound - lower_bound)\n    log_term = np.log(4.0 / delta)\n    radius = np.sqrt(2.0 * np.var(sample, ddof=1) * log_term / sample.size) + 7.0 * (upper_bound - lower_bound) * log_term / (3.0 * (sample.size - 1))\n    return float(min(upper_bound - lower_bound, radius))\n\n\ndef honest_score_intervals(outcomes, scores, unlabeled_scores, alpha, seed, affine=False):\n    \"\"\"Separate Wald and finite-sample tracks; no selection between intervals.\"\"\"\n    from scipy.special import ndtri\n\n    labeled_count = outcomes.size\n    unlabeled_count = unlabeled_scores.size\n    rho = unlabeled_count / (labeled_count + unlabeled_count)\n    n_folds = 2\n    component_delta = alpha / (2 * n_folds)\n    generator = np.random.default_rng(seed)\n    labeled_folds = np.array_split(generator.permutation(labeled_count), n_folds)\n    unlabeled_folds = np.array_split(generator.permutation(unlabeled_count), n_folds)\n    fold_weights = np.asarray([fold.size / labeled_count for fold in labeled_folds])\n    fold_estimates = []\n    fold_lowers = []\n    fold_uppers = []\n    oof_residuals = np.empty(labeled_count)\n    oof_controls = np.empty(unlabeled_count)\n    for fold_index in range(n_folds):\n        training_indices = labeled_folds[1 - fold_index]\n        heldout_indices = labeled_folds[fold_index]\n        unlabeled_indices = unlabeled_folds[fold_index]\n        train_scores = scores[training_indices]\n        train_outcomes = outcomes[training_indices]\n        heldout_scores = scores[heldout_indices]\n        heldout_outcomes = outcomes[heldout_indices]\n        heldout_unlabeled_scores = unlabeled_scores[unlabeled_indices]\n        if affine:\n            calibrator = fit_affine_calibrator(train_scores, train_outcomes)\n        else:\n            calibrator = fit_score_calibrator(train_scores, train_outcomes)\n        calibrated_labeled = np.clip(calibrator.predict(heldout_scores.reshape(-1, 1)), 0.0, 1.0)\n        calibrated_unlabeled = np.clip(calibrator.predict(heldout_unlabeled_scores.reshape(-1, 1)), 0.0, 1.0)\n        fold_estimate = np.mean(heldout_outcomes - rho * calibrated_labeled) + rho * np.mean(calibrated_unlabeled)\n        oof_residuals[heldout_indices] = heldout_outcomes - rho * calibrated_labeled\n        oof_controls[unlabeled_indices] = rho * calibrated_unlabeled\n        residual_radius = bounded_eb_radius(heldout_outcomes - rho * calibrated_labeled, -rho, 1.0, component_delta)\n        prediction_radius = bounded_eb_radius(rho * calibrated_unlabeled, 0.0, rho, component_delta)\n        fold_lower = fold_estimate - residual_radius - prediction_radius\n        fold_upper = fold_estimate + residual_radius + prediction_radius\n        fold_estimates.append(fold_estimate)\n        fold_lowers.append(fold_lower)\n        fold_uppers.append(fold_upper)\n    estimate = float(np.dot(fold_weights, fold_estimates))\n    wald_variance = np.var(oof_residuals, ddof=1) / labeled_count + np.var(oof_controls, ddof=1) / unlabeled_count\n    critical = float(ndtri(1.0 - alpha / 2.0))\n    boundary_adjustment = critical**2 / labeled_count\n    wald_center = (estimate + boundary_adjustment / 2.0) / (1.0 + boundary_adjustment)\n    radius = critical * math.sqrt(max(0.0, float(wald_variance)) + critical**2 / (4.0 * labeled_count**2)) / (1.0 + boundary_adjustment)\n    wald_lower = float(np.clip(wald_center - radius, 0.0, 1.0))\n    wald_upper = float(np.clip(wald_center + radius, 0.0, 1.0))\n    safe_lower = max(0.0, float(np.dot(fold_weights, fold_lowers)))\n    safe_upper = min(1.0, float(np.dot(fold_weights, fold_uppers)))\n    if safe_lower > safe_upper:\n        safe_lower, safe_upper = 0.0, 1.0\n    return {\n        \"estimate\": float(np.clip(estimate, 0.0, 1.0)),\n        \"wald\": (wald_lower, wald_upper),\n        \"finite_sample\": (safe_lower, safe_upper),\n    }\n\n\ndef fit_affine_calibrator(train_scores, train_outcomes):\n    \"\"\"Matched ridge/GCV ablation with the same clipping but no spline knots.\"\"\"\n    calibrator = ScoreCalibrator(nonlinear=False)\n    calibrator.fit(train_scores.reshape(-1, 1), train_outcomes)\n    return calibrator\n\n\ndef handle(request):\n    global _state\n    operation = request.get(\"op\")\n    if operation == \"reset\":\n        if set(request) != {\"op\", \"seed\"} or type(request[\"seed\"]) is not int or request[\"seed\"] < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid reset\")\n        _state = {\"seed\": request[\"seed\"], \"buffer\": bytearray(), \"chunks\": 0, \"meta\": None, \"done\": False}\n        return {\"ok\": True}\n    if _state is None or _state[\"done\"]:\n        raise ValueError(\"reset required\")\n    if operation == \"input\":\n        keys = {\"op\", \"n\", \"N\", \"alpha\", \"dtype\", \"encoding\", \"shape\", \"total_bytes\", \"chunk_count\", \"chunk_index\", \"offset\", \"data\"}\n        if set(request) != keys:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid input schema\")\n        labeled_size, unlabeled_size = request[\"n\"], request[\"N\"]\n        total = request[\"total_bytes\"]\n        if any(type(request[key]) is not int for key in (\"n\", \"N\", \"total_bytes\", \"chunk_count\", \"chunk_index\", \"offset\")):\n            raise ValueError(\"integer metadata required\")\n        if min(labeled_size, unlabeled_size) < 2 or total != 8 * (2 * labeled_size + unlabeled_size) or not 0 < total <= 4 * 1024**2:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid size\")\n        if request[\"dtype\"] != \"<f8\" or request[\"encoding\"] != \"base64\" or request[\"shape\"] != [2 * labeled_size + unlabeled_size] or request[\"alpha\"] != 0.05:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid metadata\")\n        if not 1 <= request[\"chunk_count\"] <= 4 or request[\"chunk_index\"] != _state[\"chunks\"] or request[\"chunk_index\"] >= request[\"chunk_count\"] or request[\"offset\"] != len(_state[\"buffer\"]):\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid chunk sequence\")\n        metadata = {key: request[key] for key in (\"n\", \"N\", \"alpha\", \"dtype\", \"encoding\", \"shape\", \"total_bytes\", \"chunk_count\")}\n        if _state[\"meta\"] is not None and _state[\"meta\"] != metadata:\n            raise ValueError(\"metadata changed\")\n        if not isinstance(request[\"data\"], str) or len(request[\"data\"]) > 1398104:\n            raise ValueError(\"oversized chunk\")\n        raw = base64.b64decode(request[\"data\"], validate=True)\n        if not 0 < len(raw) <= 1024**2 or len(_state[\"buffer\"]) + len(raw) > total:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid chunk size\")\n        _state[\"meta\"] = metadata\n        _state[\"buffer\"].extend(raw)\n        _state[\"chunks\"] += 1\n        return {\"ok\": True, \"received_bytes\": len(_state[\"buffer\"])}\n    if operation != \"infer\" or set(request) != {\"op\"}:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid operation\")\n    metadata = _state[\"meta\"]\n    if metadata is None or _state[\"chunks\"] != metadata[\"chunk_count\"] or len(_state[\"buffer\"]) != metadata[\"total_bytes\"]:\n        raise ValueError(\"incomplete input\")\n    from scipy.special import betaincinv\n    values = np.frombuffer(_state[\"buffer\"], dtype=\"<f8\")\n    labeled_size, unlabeled_size = metadata[\"n\"], metadata[\"N\"]\n    labels = values[:labeled_size]\n    predictions = values[labeled_size:2 * labeled_size]\n    unlabeled = values[2 * labeled_size:]\n    if not np.isfinite(values).all() or np.any(values < 0) or np.any(values > 1) or np.any((labels != 0) & (labels != 1)):\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid array values\")\n    count = int(labels.sum())\n    alpha = metadata[\"alpha\"]\n    if labeled_size < 16:\n        estimate = float(labels.mean())\n        lower = 0.0 if count == 0 else float(betaincinv(count, labeled_size - count + 1, alpha / 2))\n        upper = 1.0 if count == labeled_size else float(betaincinv(count + 1, labeled_size - count, 1 - alpha / 2))\n    else:\n        intervals = honest_score_intervals(labels, predictions, unlabeled, alpha, _state[\"seed\"])\n        estimate = intervals[\"estimate\"]\n        lower, upper = intervals[\"wald\"]\n    _state[\"done\"] = True\n    _state[\"buffer\"] = bytearray()\n    return {\"estimate\": float(estimate), \"lower\": lower, \"upper\": upper}\n# Honest score-only controls: implementation status\n\n## Contract and primary output\n\nThe target remains the binary superpopulation mean E[Y]. The only inputs are\nthe supplied labeled outcomes and frozen labeled/unlabeled scalar scores.\nThe predictor is not retrained. `program.py:handle` keeps the starter's\nreset/input/infer schemas, byte assembly, state transitions, and output bounds.\nNo diagnostic fields or track selector are added to RPC responses. There are\nno additional runtime dependencies beyond the starter's NumPy and SciPy.\n\nThe primary RPC interval is a stabilized asymptotic Wald interval for n >= 16. For\nn < 16 it is the labeled-only Clopper-Pearson interval; this rule uses sample\nsize only, not labels, fitted widths, scores, or hidden truths. The primary\nprocedure is NOT an exact finite-sample procedure for general n. In particular,\nrare outcomes, all-zero/all-one samples, unstable fitted controls, and a fixed\nsmall sample can invalidate Wald coverage. A zero empirical variance is not\nevidence of a known population mean. To avoid a mechanically zero-width\ninterval on constant outcomes, let z be the normal critical value and a=z^2/n.\nThe interval center is (mu_hat+a/2)/(1+a), and its radius is\nz*sqrt(V_hat+z^2/(4*n^2))/(1+a). This deterministic Wilson-style stabilization\nis first-order equivalent to Wald at a nondegenerate limit, but is NOT an exact\nWilson interval for a fitted control estimator and has no finite-sample\ncoverage claim. It does not select between methods or tracks using outcomes.\n\n## Fold isolation and calibration\n\nTwo random, label-independent labeled folds and two unlabeled folds are formed\nfrom the reset seed. For each evaluation fold, only the complementary labeled\nscores and labels enter `fit_score_calibrator`. Neither held-out labels nor\nheld-out scores nor any unlabeled scores determine knots, scaling, penalties,\nor the fitted intercept. Fit-time knots are training quantiles. Candidate\nfamilies are an intercept, a ridge affine function, and small piecewise-linear\nsplines; training GCV chooses their penalties and complexity. The maximum\nnumber of interior knots is seven. Held-out predictions are clipped to [0,1].\n`fit_affine_calibrator` uses the same ridge penalties, GCV, constant candidate,\nand clipping but excludes knots; `honest_score_intervals(..., affine=True)` is\nan internal matched ablation, not a new RPC input. Clipped affine controls are\nnot the same class as unrestricted population-optimal affine controls.\n\nFold estimates are mean(Y-rho*g(S)) + rho*mean(g(S_U)), rho=N/(n+N).\nFold weights are the fixed labeled fold sizes divided by n, and are shared\nbetween the point estimate and finite-sample endpoints. The two variance terms\nuse the separate labeled residual and unlabeled control arrays. Odd sample\nsizes yield slightly unequal fold weights; the pooled Wald variance is a\nfirst-order approximation, not an exact finite-sample variance identity.\n\n## Separate finite-sample safety track\n\n`honest_score_intervals` also computes `finite_sample` internally. The RPC\ndoes not expose it, compare its width to Wald, intersect it with Wald, or\nselect a track using its width. It is available for a future permitted paired\nstudy through direct internal function calls; no such study was run here.\n\nAssume independent iid labeled pairs and iid unlabeled scores from the same\npopulation, and a predictor frozen independently of evaluation labels. Given\neach fold's complementary training sample, its held-out residuals have support\n[-rho,1] and its held-out controls have support [0,rho]. Apply the two-sided\nempirical-Bernstein radius\n\n    sqrt(2*s^2*log(4/delta)/m) + 7*B*log(4/delta)/(3*(m-1))\n\nwith unbiased variance and known range length B. The radius is capped by B,\nwhich remains valid because both sample and population means are in support.\nFor m=1, a full-range radius gives support fallback. The interface guarantees\nn,N>=2, so neither of the two held-out folds is empty. Each of 2K components\nreceives delta=alpha/(2K). A union bound gives simultaneous coverage regardless\nof dependence across fitted folds. Averaging endpoints with the same fixed\nnonnegative weights therefore covers E[Y] with probability at least 1-alpha.\nOnly the known outcome support [0,1] is used to clip the resulting interval.\nAn empty numerical intersection is conservatively replaced by [0,1]. This\nargument does not assume unconditional independence of cross-fitted folds.\nThe safety interval can be much wider than Wald or Clopper-Pearson.\n\n## Oracle frontier and asymptotic qualifications\n\nFor fixed g, write m(S)=E[Y|S], rho=N/(n+N). Then\n\n    Var(mu_hat_g) = Var(Y-rho*g(S))/n + rho^2*Var(g(S))/N\n    Var(mu_hat_m) = E[Var(Y|S)]/n + Var(m(S))/(n+N).\n\nThe excess variance relative to m is rho/n times Var(g(S)-m(S)); additive\nconstants in g cancel. The reduction over the best unrestricted affine score\ncontrol is N/[n(n+N)] times E[(m-Pi_affine(m))^2]. For constant scores the affine\nprojection is the intercept. `study.py:oracle_frontier` implements this formula\nfrom supplied analytic moments, not hidden evaluation targets.\n\nFor the Wald approximation, require n,N tending to infinity with a positive\nfinite limiting ratio, a nondegenerate limiting variance, and fold calibrators\nconverging in L2 to the same deterministic bounded function. Conditional\ncentering and cross-fitting then make nuisance empirical-mean errors negligible\nrelative to n^(-1/2). This is not an assertion of finite-fold independence.\nThe fixed spline dictionary need not consistently estimate arbitrary m, so\nattainment of the oracle frontier is not claimed. Nonlinear information,\nenough training labels, and stable nuisance estimation are needed for gains.\nAffine m, uninformative scores, small N, rare events, and nuisance estimation\ncosts are important no-gain or failure regimes. Distribution shift is excluded.\n\n## Evidence and unfinished work\n\nOnly permitted static candidate/interface inspection and editing occurred.\nNo candidate code was executed or imported; no tests, benchmarks, searches,\npackage installs, data downloads, or evaluations were run. No prohibited\nliterature was encountered. No literature outside the provided task text was\nread. No empirically measured coverage, gain, timing, or score is reported.\nThe fold isolation above is a source-level design audit, not an executed test.\n\n`study.py` supplies unexecuted paired uncertainty and per-repetition record\nhelpers: exact binomial coverage intervals, paired t intervals for mean width\ndifferences, and explicit validity-track labels. They are not a completed\nexperiment runner. Paired repetitions, independent synthetic truth checks,\nreal-data masking studies, established-baseline comparisons, executable audit\ntests, and the conference paper/PDF remain uncompleted because this stage\nallows candidate preparation only and explicitly forbids their execution or\nexternal source access. The harness alone performs the official evaluation.\n\"\"\"Offline mathematical/reporting utilities, not additional RPC operations.\n\nThese helpers have not been executed in this stage. They do not generate or\nclaim empirical evidence and are never called by candidate inference. All\ncomparisons must use identical observations and repetition identifiers.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport math\n\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import binomtest, t\n\n\ndef oracle_frontier(labeled_count, unlabeled_count, conditional_noise_variance,\n                    regression_variance, nonlinear_projection_error):\n    \"\"\"Population moments must be known analytically, not estimated from truth leaks.\"\"\"\n    if min(labeled_count, unlabeled_count) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"two nonempty variance samples required\")\n    if min(conditional_noise_variance, regression_variance, nonlinear_projection_error) < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"negative variance moment\")\n    if nonlinear_projection_error > regression_variance + 1e-12:\n        raise ValueError(\"projection error exceeds regression variance\")\n    oracle_variance = conditional_noise_variance / labeled_count + regression_variance / (labeled_count + unlabeled_count)\n    oracle_gain = unlabeled_count * nonlinear_projection_error / (labeled_count * (labeled_count + unlabeled_count))\n    return {\"oracle_variance\": float(oracle_variance),\n            \"best_affine_variance\": float(oracle_variance + oracle_gain),\n            \"oracle_gain\": float(oracle_gain)}\n\n\ndef paired_uncertainty(covered, candidate_widths, baseline_widths):\n    \"\"\"Exact binomial coverage CI and paired t width-difference CI across iid runs.\"\"\"\n    covered = np.asarray(covered, dtype=bool)\n    candidate_widths = np.asarray(candidate_widths, dtype=float)\n    baseline_widths = np.asarray(baseline_widths, dtype=float)\n    if covered.ndim != 1 or candidate_widths.shape != covered.shape or baseline_widths.shape != covered.shape or covered.size < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"at least two paired repetitions required\")\n    if not np.isfinite(candidate_widths).all() or not np.isfinite(baseline_widths).all() or np.any(candidate_widths < 0) or np.any(baseline_widths < 0):\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid widths\")\n    coverage_count = int(np.count_nonzero(covered))\n    coverage_interval = binomtest(coverage_count, len(covered)).proportion_ci(confidence_level=0.95, method='exact')\n    paired_width_difference = candidate_widths - baseline_widths\n    difference_mean = float(np.mean(paired_width_difference))\n    difference_se = float(np.std(paired_width_difference, ddof=1) / math.sqrt(len(covered)))\n    critical = float(t.ppf(0.975, len(covered) - 1))\n    return {\n        \"repetitions\": len(covered),\n        \"coverage_count\": coverage_count,\n        \"coverage\": float(np.mean(covered)),\n        \"coverage_interval\": [float(coverage_interval.low), float(coverage_interval.high)],\n        \"mean_width\": float(np.mean(candidate_widths)),\n        \"mean_width_se\": float(np.std(candidate_widths, ddof=1) / math.sqrt(len(covered))),\n        \"baseline_mean_width\": float(np.mean(baseline_widths)),\n        \"paired_width_difference\": difference_mean,\n        \"paired_width_difference_interval\": [difference_mean - critical * difference_se,\n                                             difference_mean + critical * difference_se],\n    }\n\n\ndef repetition_record(repetition, method, estimate, lower, upper, validity_track):\n    \"\"\"Serializable per-repetition record; caller controls persistence, not RPC.\"\"\"\n    if validity_track not in {\"asymptotic\", \"finite_sample\", \"oracle_asymptotic\"}:\n        raise ValueError(\"unknown validity track\")\n    record = {\"repetition\": int(repetition), \"method\": str(method),\n              \"estimate\": float(estimate), \"lower\": float(lower),\n              \"upper\": float(upper), \"width\": float(upper - lower)}\n    record['validity_track'] = validity_track\n    return record\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
