{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_153","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport subprocess\npath=Path('paper.tex');text=path.read_text(); old='When comparing fixed-input intervals, evaluate coverage before treating smaller width as useful. Use paired differences when comparator widths can vanish, keep a finite-sample reference near the boundary, and avoid selecting an interval after inspecting its realized width unless selection itself is covered by the inference argument. Here, those principles reveal a modest local width gain, frequent widening against the stronger prediction-assisted comparator, and a sparse-outcome comparison whose apparent efficiency changes once exact inference is included. They motivate controlled follow-up rather than an unsupported general recommendation.'\nnew='For fixed-input comparisons, assess coverage before interpreting width. Paired differences remain meaningful when comparator widths vanish, and exact inference supplies an important boundary reference. Selecting an interval after observing its realized width requires its own coverage argument. Here, these principles reveal a modest local gain and substantial comparator dependence, not a generally preferable interval. They motivate controlled follow-up rather than a deployment recommendation.'\npatch='*** Begin Patch\\\\n*** Update File: paper.tex\\\\n@@\\\\n-'+old+'\\\\n+'+new+'\\\\n*** End Patch\\\\n';subprocess.run(['/home/agent-home/.codex/tmp/arg0/codex-arg04CM673/apply_patch',patch],check=True)\nPY\n/home/agent-home/.codex/tmp/arg0/codex-arg04CM673/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: paper.tex\n@@\n-\\\\includegraphics[width=\\\\textwidth]{figures/frozen_score_overview.png}\n+\\\\begin{minipage}[c][1.8in][c]{\\\\textwidth}\n+\\\\centering\n+\\\\includegraphics[width=\\\\textwidth,height=1.8in,keepaspectratio]{figures/frozen_score_overview.png}\n+\\\\end{minipage}\n*** Add File: scripts/cap