{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_169","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport subprocess\nsource=Path('paper.tex').read_text()\nchanges={\n'The contributions are therefore threefold: a precise separation of fixed-score efficiency potential from the assumptions needed for inference; a complete, paired comparison retaining favorable and unfavorable conditions; and a failure analysis showing why non-collapse, narrowness, and validity are different properties. The manuscript deliberately preserves the archive\\\\'s limits: unidentified condition generators cannot be renamed as familiar datasets, and unavailable controlled ablations cannot be replaced by a mechanistic improvement claim.':\n'The contributions are threefold: separating fixed-score efficiency potential from inference assumptions; a complete paired comparison retaining favorable and unfavorable conditions; and a failure analysis distinguishing non-collapse, narrowness, and validity. Missing generators and controlled ablations remain explicit limitations rather than being filled with inferred mechanisms.',\n'The records do not supply condition generators, dataset names, per-condition \"'$n$ or $N$, raw scores, or label arrays. We therefore cannot identify which conditions have nonlinear calibration, distribution shift, or particular predictor quality. Even though the target truths are recorded, they do not identify the data-generating mechanisms. All conditions are included; there is no evidence of a separately named transfer task or an additional task-specific result set. Accordingly, this is a paired evaluation-condition study, not evidence of cross-domain generalization.'\"':\n'The records omit condition generators, dataset names, per-condition \"'$n,N$, raw scores, and label arrays. Recorded truths do not identify calibration nonlinearity, distribution shift, or predictor quality. All conditions are included, but no separately named transfer task is evidenced. These are evaluation-condition comparisons, not established cross-domain generalization.'\"',\n'Comparator names describe the supplied endpoint records. Their precise library versions and internal fitting details are unavailable, so empirical conclusions apply to those evaluated comparators. The archived calibration source is readable and its entrypoint is declared in the accompanying specification. However, a separately staged final source tree is unavailable for file-by-file identity verification. We distinguish the source-level method description from the sealed numerical results rather than treating a hash or a matching method family as proof of exact implementation identity.':\n'Comparator versions and internal fitting details are unavailable; conclusions apply to the supplied endpoints. The archived calibration source and entrypoint are readable, but a separate final source tree is unavailable for file-by-file identity verification. Source-level method descriptions are therefore distinguished from sealed numerical results: neither a hash nor method-family alignment proves exact implementation identity.',\n'These intervals assume an appropriate repeated-sampling interpretation of repetitions; distinct seeds alone do not verify it. They are pointwise, not simultaneous across conditions or comparators. Multiplicity changes the inferential question \\\\\\\\citep{BenjaminiHochberg1995}; we neither perform a multiple-testing procedure nor describe the isolated favorable comparison as a familywise-certified gain. We report the full collection to avoid selection by sign. Because conditions within a seed may share randomness, the pooled coverage is descriptive rather than a binomial estimate from independent condition--repetition pairs.':\n'These intervals assume a repeated-sampling interpretation that distinct seeds alone do not verify. They are pointwise, not simultaneous. Multiplicity changes the question \\\\\\\\citep{BenjaminiHochberg1995}; no multiple-testing procedure or familywise gain claim is made. All comparisons are retained regardless of sign. Conditions within a seed may share randomness, so pooled coverage is descriptive, not a binomial estimate from independent condition--repetition pairs.'\n}\npatch='*** Begin Patch\\\\n*** Update File: paper.tex\\\\n'\nfor old,new in changes.items():\n assert old in source, old[:90]\n patch+='@@\\\\n-'+old+'\\\\n+'+new+'\\\\n'\npatch+='*** End Patch\\\\n';subprocess.run(['/home/agent-home/.codex/tmp/arg0/codex-arg04CM673/apply_patch',patch],check=True)\nPY\npython .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py run -- pdflatex -recorder -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex > compilation.log 2>&1\npython .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_compile_inputs.py run -- pdflatex -recorder -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error paper.tex > compilation.log 2>&1\npython .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_tex_refs.py --tex paper.tex --bib references.bib\npython .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts/validate_claims.py --paper-data paper_data.json --references references_provenance.json --bib references.bib --claims claims_grounding.json --paper-dir .\n/tmp/paper-tools/bin/python scripts/check_paper.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
